---
// Bare frame-rate test page (not linked anywhere, hidden from search engines)
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <meta name="robots" content="noindex">
  <title>Frame rate test</title>
  <style>
    body { margin: 0; font: 16px/1.5 -apple-system, system-ui, sans-serif; background: #F6F5F8; color: #121116; }
    main { max-width: 640px; margin: 80px auto; padding: 0 20px; }
    .box { width: 60px; height: 60px; border-radius: 14px; background: #6B4BFF; margin: 40px 0; animation: move 2s ease-in-out infinite alternate; }
    @keyframes move { to { transform: translateX(300px); } }
    #fps { position: fixed; left: 12px; bottom: 12px; padding: 10px 14px; border-radius: 10px; color: #fff; font: 600 14px/1.4 ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace; background: #333; }
    .spacer { height: 3000px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <main>
    <h1>Frame rate test</h1>
    <p>This page has nothing on it except one moving square. If this also shows ~30 fps, your browser is limiting the frame rate (not the website).</p>
    <div class="box"></div>
    <p>Your screen refresh rate guess: <b id="hz">measuring…</b></p>
    <div class="spacer"></div>
  </main>
  <div id="fps">…</div>
  <script is:inline src="/dev/fps-test.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
